        "id": "how-i-fit-django-into-a-turborepo-without-fighting-it",
        "slug": "how-i-fit-django-into-a-turborepo-without-fighting-it",
        "status": "published",
        "data": {
          "title": "How I Fit Django Into a Turborepo Without Fighting It",
          "published_date": "2026-01-10",
          "summary": "When people talk about Turborepo, most examples assume you are adding another JavaScript app. Here is how I brought Django in cleanly.",
          "content": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "When people talk about Turborepo, most examples assume you are adding another JavaScript app. That was not my situation. I needed to bring a Django backend into the same repo as a Next.js web app and a React Native mobile app. Once I used it correctly, integrating Django stopped feeling like a workaround and started feeling intentional.",
                  "_key": "k1"
                }
              ],
              "_key": "k0"
            }
          ]
        },
        "taxonomies": {
          "tag": [
            "full-stack"
